00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:52.000
And I started to wonder, what is this organization, where did it come from and why are they taking charge of public space? Sharon says that from the beginning, bids weren't just planting beautiful flowers and sweeping the streets. They played a big role in New York's gentrification. Under Mayor Giuliani, for example, New York City started cracking down on petty crimes, disproportionately arresting New Yorkers of color. You probably know this as broken windows style policing,
我开始想，这个组织是什么，它是从哪里来的，为什么他们要接管公共空间？莎朗说，从一开始，商业改进区就不只是在种植美丽的花朵和清扫街道。它们在纽约的士绅化进程中发挥了重要作用。例如，在朱利安尼市长执政期间，纽约市开始打击轻微犯罪，不成比例地逮捕有色人种纽约人。你可能知道这就是破窗风格的警务，

48
00:24:52.000 --> 00:25:23.000
and bids were a key part of enforcing it. They rousted homeless people from sleeping in various places in their territory, and some of them in midtown Manhattan hired homeless people like to chase other homeless people out of these spaces. And it wasn't a good situation. In the past 30 years, a lot of bids have dialed back some of their sketchiest security practices. But some haven't.
而商业改进区是执行这一政策的关键部分。他们在领地内的各个地方驱赶睡觉的无家可归者，曼哈顿中城的一些商业改进区甚至雇佣无家可归者去把其他无家可归者赶出这些空间。这不是一个好情况。在过去的30年里，许多商业改进区已经缩减了他们的一些最令人反感的安保做法。但有些没有。

49
00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:55.000
Bids operating around the country today have written exclusion orders to keep people out of certain neighborhoods and used high-tech surveillance to monitor public space, all with mixed results as to whether any of this actually yields a meaningful drop in violent crime. The tricky thing about bids is that they aren't forces for good or forces for bad. They are, above all, forces of capital. Most of the choices they make and the things they do come down to one question. How can we make this area nice enough that people want to spend their time and money here?
今天在全国各地运营的商业改进区已经写好了驱逐令，将人们赶出某些街区，并使用高科技监控来监视公共空间，但对于这是否真的能带来暴力犯罪的有意义下降，其效果褒贬不一。商业改进区棘手的地方在于，它们既不是纯粹的正义力量，也不是纯粹的邪恶力量。最重要的是，它们是资本的力量。它们做出的绝大多数选择和事情都归结为一个问题。我们如何能把这个地区变得足够好，让人愿意在这里花时间和金钱？

50
00:25:55.000 --> 00:26:46.000
The problem is that this is a private group. These bids are responsible for keeping property values at the same level or even raising property values because that's what building owners are interested in. That means that the business improvement districts are not concerned if rents rise, if commercial rents rise. And while these higher commercial rents might benefit property owners, they can push out small local businesses. Small stores are forced out of business because they can't pay those rents. If you own a small barber shop or craft shop or gym yoga studio or something, the Business Improvement District is not that interested in you. Here's Daniel Kudla again.
问题在于这是一个私人集团。这些商业改进区负责保持财产价值处于同一水平甚至提高财产价值，因为这正是业主们感兴趣的。这意味着商业改进区并不关心租金上涨，如果商业租金上涨的话。虽然这些较高的商业租金可能会让业主受益，但它们可能会挤走当地的小企业。小商店被迫停业，因为他们付不起这些租金。如果你经营一家小理发店、工艺品店、健身瑜伽工作室或类似的东西，商业改进区其实并不那么对你感兴趣。这是丹尼尔·库德拉再次发言。

51
00:26:46.000 --> 00:27:14.000
There's definitely a tie between increased gentrification in a lot of mid-sized larger North American cities coinciding with the formation of bids. Officially bids report to the city, but recent audits show that some cities aren't keeping as close tabs on bids as they're supposed to. And if the public has any opinion on that, there's not much they can do. Because bids are private groups. Their members can't get voted out on election day. But of course, not everyone agrees with bid critics.
在中小型和大型北美城市的士绅化加剧与商业改进区的成立之间，绝对存在着联系。官方上商业改进区向市政府报告，但最近的审计显示，一些城市并没有像它们应该的那样密切关注商业改进区。如果公众对此有任何意见，他们也无能为力。因为商业改进区是私人群体。它们的成员无法在选举日被投票下台。当然，并非所有人都同意商业改进区批评者的看法。

52
00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:48.000
Tom Harris of the Times Square Alliance bid says a lot of those critiques of policing, of minimal oversight, don't land anymore. I would say it's completely the opposite of do what these property owners say to do. The mayor has a rep on our board. The controller has a rep on our board. We have resident reps. And look, if you live in Times Square, you're not shy. And our residents tend to dominate our meetings because they care about their neighborhood the way everyone cares about their neighborhood.
时代广场联盟商业改进区的汤姆·哈里斯表示，关于警务、监督不足的许多批评已经不再成立了。我要说，这完全与听从这些业主的话相反。市长在我们的董事会有代表。审计长在我们的董事会有代表。我们有居民代表。而且听着，如果你住在时代广场，你可不会害羞。我们的居民往往在我们的会议上占主导地位，因为他们像其他人关心自己的街区一样关心他们的街区。

53
00:27:48.000 --> 00:28:09.000
The Times Square bid is actually known as one of New York's more inclusive creative bids. Unlike some of the more heavy-handed ones, the Times Square Alliance doesn't simply relocate unhoused people. Instead, they connect them with nearby resources, and they've done this more or less from the beginning. In the 90s, they helped open a supportive housing hotel for folks living on the street
时代广场商业改进区实际上被认为是纽约更具包容性、更有创意的商业改进区之一。与一些手段更严厉的商业改进区不同，时代广场联盟不仅简单地重新安置无家可归者。相反，他们将他们与附近的资源联系起来，而且他们基本上从一开始就这么做了。在90年代，他们帮助在时代广场为露宿街头的人们开设了一家支持性住房酒店。

54
00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:37.000
in Times Square. Lauren Henry Tom also says that bids are able to take action on the needs of their specific neighborhood. Tom Biddle Bids are a little bit more nimble and can move quicker than the city government. We're sort of like a wave runner and the city is a cruise ship. So we can move a little bit more quickly and we can pilot things and see if they work a lot easier than the city can. Bids often get things done and fast.
劳伦·亨利·汤姆还表示，商业改进区能够针对他们特定街区的需求采取行动。汤姆·比德商业改进区比市政府更灵活、行动更快。我们有点像水上摩托艇，而城市就像一艘游轮。所以我们可以行动得更快一些，并且可以试行一些事情，比城市更容易看出它们是否奏效。商业改进区通常能把事情办成，而且速度很快。

55
00:28:37.000 --> 00:29:04.000
Today, in addition to working with the city to host New Year's Eve, the Times Square Alliance puts on 80 live shows a year. They run a public art project where every night at midnight, 364 days a year, a local artist takes over all the billboards in Times Square. It's cool. And back in 2009, when the city wanted to turn the busiest stretch of Broadway into a pedestrian street for much of the day, they asked the Times Square bid to help spearhead that project.
今天，除了与城市合作举办新年夜活动之外，时代广场联盟每年举办80场现场演出。他们运营着一个公共艺术项目，每年364天，每天午夜，当地艺术家都会接管时代广场的所有广告牌。这很酷。早在2009年，当市政府希望将百老汇最繁忙的一段在一天中的大部分时间变成步行街时，他们请时代广场联盟协助牵头该项目。

56
00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:35.000
And they did. Pedestrianizing Broadway has been probably one of the greatest things for public space in the last 40 years. Before that, Times Square was two streets that crossed and a lot of signs. Now it's a vibrant public space and we created a destination. In short, bids are a paradox. They exist ultimately to serve businesses and property owners. They're not democratic institutions.
他们确实做到了。将百老汇步行化可能是过去40年来对公共空间做过的最伟大的事情之一。在此之前，时代广场是两条交叉的街道和许多广告牌。现在它是一个充满活力的公共空间，我们创造了一个目的地。简而言之，商业改进区是一个悖论。它们的存在最终是为了服务于企业和业主。它们不是民主机构。

57
00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:59.000
And yet they are responsible for a lot of the special things that can make a city feel like home. I had that experience myself. One of my good friends, who's a poet in California was visiting. I walked with her through Bryant Park in Midtown. And I said, look at this. Isn't this awful? The business improvement district runs this public space as though it's their private
然而，它们却负责了许多能让城市感觉像家的特殊事物。我自己也有过这种经历。我的一位好朋友，一位在加利福尼亚的诗人来访。我和她一起穿过中城的布莱恩特公园。我说，看看这个。这不是很糟糕吗？商业改进区把这个公共空间当成他们自己的私人空间来管理。

58
00:29:59.000 --> 00:30:14.000
space. And she said, what's bad? There are lovely restaurants. People are sitting on the benches and Rita Dove, who was the US poet laureate at that time, was going to give a noonday poetry reading. So my friend said, what's wrong with you? This is great.
她说，哪里不好了？这里有可爱的餐馆。人们坐在长椅上，而当时担任美国桂冠诗人的丽塔·多夫将要在这里进行一场中午的诗歌朗诵。所以我朋友说，你脑子有毛病吧？这太棒了。

59
00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:42.000
Who cares who's paying for it? Bids are addressing a failure of the system. A lot of things that bids do, public events, nice decorations, these are things that cities often can't or at least don't pay for. The question is whether private businesses with ill-defined authorities should be the ones filling in that gap. It's in a way fortunate that Mr. Moneybags comes in to pay for things
谁在乎是谁在出资？商业促进区（BID）正在解决系统失效的问题。许多BID所做的事情，比如公共活动、精美的装饰，都是城市往往无力承担、或者至少不愿出资去做的事情。问题在于，这些权限界定不清的私营企业是否应该成为填补这一空白的主体。从某种程度上说，有钱人来为我们在公共空间中真正需要的东西买单是一件幸运的事。

60
00:30:42.000 --> 00:31:17.000
that we really need in public space. But I don't think we have a full democracy in a city if we allow private organizations to control who can use public space. We already have a way of paying into shared space. And that's taxation, a city government. If companies paid into the government instead of the bid, that money could go to amenities and neighborhoods all over the city. But bids have become so ingrained in our political process, that's unlikely to happen anytime soon.
但我不认为，如果我们允许私人组织控制谁能使用公共空间，这座城市就能实现真正的民主。我们其实已经有了一种为共享空间出资的方式，那就是税收和市政府。如果企业把钱交个政府而不是BID，这笔资金就可以用于全市各个街区的公共设施。但在我们的政治进程中，BID已经根深蒂固，这种情况在短期内不太可能会发生。

61
00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:47.000
Perhaps the thing that should make us the most squeamish about all this is just how much a bid looks like a mini-democracy. They vote, they tax themselves, and they decide how to use that money. But it's property owners who get the loudest say. In this country, we tried that before, having a democracy where the landowners were the only ones who had a voice. And we left it behind for a very good reason. Whatever you feel about bids, one thing they do show is that there is an appetite for exciting,
也许最让人感到不安的是，BID看起来就像是一个微型民主体制。它们投票、自我征税，并决定如何使用这笔资金。但拥有最大话语权的是业主。在这个国家，我们以前尝试过这种让地主拥有发言权的民主形式，而我们抛弃它是出于非常充分的理由。无论你对BID作何感想，它们至少表明了一点，那就是人们对令人兴奋的、雄心勃勃的，当然有时也是代价高昂的城市规划项目有着强烈的需求。

62
00:31:47.000 --> 00:32:34.000
ambitious, and yes, sometimes costly, urban planning projects. I mean, just look at New Year's Eve in Times Square. Love it or hate it, it's nothing if not testimony to the fact that people want to come together. In celebration of the slow descent of the ball and a rocking display of confetti, sure. But also in celebration of urban space, where we can get together on the streets and sidewalks that we publicly share. 99% Invisible was reported this week by Katie Thornton, edited by Kelly Prime with help from Vivienne Leigh, mixed by Martín González, music by Swan Real, fact-checking by Graham Hayesha.
我是说，看看时代广场的跨年夜就知道了。无论你喜欢还是讨厌它，它都充分证明了一个事实：人们渴望聚在一起。当然，是为了庆祝水晶球的缓缓降落和震撼人心的彩带喷洒，但也是为了庆祝城市空间，在这里我们可以在共同拥有的街道和人行道上欢聚。本周的《99% Invisible》由凯蒂·桑顿（Katie Thornton）报道，凯利·普莱姆（Kelly Prime）编辑，薇维安·利（Vivienne Leigh）协助，马丁·冈萨雷斯（Martín González）混音，斯旺·里尔（Swan Real）配乐，格雷厄姆·海耶沙（Graham Hayesha）核实事实。

63
00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:59.000
For more about the queer history of Times Square, check out the book Times Square Red, Times Square Blue by Samuel Delaney. Cathy Tu is our executive producer, Kurt Kohlstedt is the digital director, Delaney Hall is our senior editor. The rest of the team includes Chris Berube, Jason DeLeon, Emmett Fitzgerald, Christopher Johnson, Lashma Dawn, Joe Rosenberg, Gabriella Gladney, Jacob Medina Gleason, me Roman Mars, and Nina Potuck. This is Nina's last episode with us.
想了解更多关于时代广场的酷儿历史，请阅读塞缪尔·德莱尼（Samuel Delaney）的书《时代广场红，时代广场蓝》。凯西·图（Cathy Tu）是我们的执行制片人，库尔特·科尔斯特ッド（Kurt Kohlstedt）是数字总监，德莱尼·霍尔（Delaney Hall）是我们的高级编辑。团队其他成员包括克里斯·贝鲁布（Chris Berube）、杰森·德莱昂（Jason DeLeon）、埃米特·菲茨杰拉德（Emmett Fitzgerald）、克里斯托弗·约翰逊（Christopher Johnson）、拉什玛·道恩（Lashma Dawn）、乔·罗森伯格（Joe Rosenberg）、加布里埃拉·格拉德尼（Gabriella Gladney）、雅各布·梅迪纳·格利森（Jacob Medina Gleason）、还有我罗曼·马尔斯（Roman Mars）以及尼娜·波图克（Nina Potuck）。这是尼娜在我们这里的最后期节目。

64
00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:20.000
She's been filling in for this year and just doing an amazing job. We were so lucky to have spent time with her. So thank you, Nina, and I'm sure we will be working together again very soon. The 99% of his logo was created by Stefan Lawrence. We are part of the Stitcher and SiriusXM podcast family, now headquartered six blocks north in the Pandora building
她今年一直在代班，而且表现极其出色。我们非常幸运能和她共度这段时光。所以谢谢你，尼娜，我相信我们很快还会再次合作。99%的标志是由斯特凡·劳伦斯（Stefan Lawrence）创作的。我们是Stitcher和SiriusXM播客家族的一部分，总部现在位于美丽的加利福尼亚州奥克兰市中心以北六个街区的潘多拉大厦。

